index 0000000..1b29a46
--- /dev/null
+++ b/skills/trailer-maker/SKILL.md
@@ -0,0 +1,52 @@
+---
+name: trailer-maker
+description: Makes a game trailer for a Unity game end to end - a beat sheet from the game's hook, gameplay captures recorded in the Editor, title/end cards rendered in Unity, music/VO/SFX from ElevenLabs (or a licensed track), an edit list rendered by ffmpeg, and two independent reviews (pacing, technical) before delivery. Use when someone wants a trailer, teaser, gameplay reel, store-page video, Steam/itch capsule video, or a short clip for social media, even if they only say "make a video of my game".
+---
+
+# trailer-maker
+
+A trailer is an edit list, not a timeline you hand-tweak: plan beats, capture shots, generate audio, write `edl.json`, render it, review it, fix the list, render again.
+
+`<skill>` = this skill's folder. `T` = the trailer folder, default `Trailer/<yyyy-mm-dd>/` in the project root (outside `Assets/`, so Unity never imports it).
+
+## When to use / not to use
+- Use for: trailers, teasers, gameplay reels, store videos (Steam 16:9, vertical 9:16 for social), recut of an existing trailer.
+- Not for: in-game cutscenes (Timeline in Unity), screenshots for store pages (play-verify), builds (build-ship).
+
+## Requirements
+- ffmpeg with ffprobe. `python3 <skill>/scripts/render_trailer.py check` says what it found and what is missing. Text is drawn on cards
+in Unity (with your game's font), so the slim Homebrew formula is enough; `ffmpeg-full` adds `drawtext`/libass if
+you want burned-in subtitles. Set `TRAILER_FFMPEG` to use a specific ffmpeg binary or folder.
+- Unity Recorder (`com.unity.recorder`) for captures; ElevenLabs for generated audio (optional).
+
+## Workflow
+1. **Beat sheet** (`T/BEATS.md`): length (30/60/90 s), the hook in one line, then beats with target seconds:
+   cold open (strongest shot, 0-3 s), premise, mechanics escalating, a breather, climax, title card, call to action
+   (wishlist / release date). Show it to the user before capturing anything.
+2. **Captures** (`T/capture/`): one clip per shot with Unity Recorder (MP4, H.264, the trailer's resolution and fps,
+   1.5 s handles on both ends). Play-mode scripted or played by the user; name clips after beats (`03-boss-dash.mp4`).
+   Unity Recorder's movie output **doesn't capture its audio**: record game audio separately or rebuild it in the mix (references/audio.md).
+   Probe every clip: `python3 <skill>/scripts/render_trailer.py probe T/capture/*.mp4`.
+3. **Cards** (`T/cards/`): title, logo, release date, call to action as PNGs at the trailer resolution, rendered from
+   a Unity scene or UI canvas so they use the game's font and palette.
+4. **Audio** (`T/audio/`, details in references/audio.md): music via the `music` tool with a `composition_plan` whose
+sections match the beat sheet, VO via `tts`, SFX via `sound_effect`; copy files to `T/audio/`, record items in
+   REPORT.md. Before generating anything, show the plan, the voices and the estimated credits to the user
+for approval. Reuse items instead of regenerating; record what you used in `audio/sources.json`:
+   `{"file": "audio/music.mp3", "item_id": "itm_…", "tool": "music", "credits": 1500, "prompt_or_plan": …}`.
+A licensed track works the same way from step 3 (record the license in REPORT.md).
+5. **Edit list** (`T/edl.json`): clips with in/out points cut on the music's section boundaries, cards with durations,
+   audio tracks with start times. Format in the script's docstring. Dry run first:
+   `python3 <skill>/scripts/render_trailer.py render T/edl.json --dry-run`.
+6. **Render**: `python3 <skill>/scripts/render_trailer.py render T/edl.json`. The script checks the output's
+   duration against the edit list; `RESULT: OK` is the only proof the render worked.
+7. **Review**: spawn the two reviewers in references/reviewers.md as separate subagents, each given only the edit
+   list, the beat sheet, probe output and contact frames (`ffmpeg -i out.mp4 -vf fps=1,scale=480:-1 T/frames/%03d.png`),
+   not your opinion. Fix what they find in `edl.json` (not by hand-editing video) and render again. At most 3 rounds.
+8. **Deliver** `T/REPORT.md`: output path, length, resolution, audio sources and credits spent, review verdicts,
+   anything left open. The rendered MP4 shows up under Items.
+
+## Rules
+- Never overwrite a delivered render: version outputs (`out/trailer-v2.mp4`).
+- Every shot is real gameplay from the current build; say so in REPORT.md if any shot is staged.
+- Ask before spending more than the confirm threshold on audio, and before regenerating approved audio.
